Problems with the Chevy Cruze have been reported to overshadow the benefits of this particular model. The Cruze’s advertising boasts excellent gas mileage, handling, and longevity. However, it has been reported to also be known in the auto world as a vehicle full of manufacturing defects.

Unfortunately, the number of Chevrolet Cruze problems serves as a cautionary tale to consumers in California looking for protection from problems with California’s Lemon Law.

Are you facing any issues with your 2019-2021 Chevy Cruze?

If so, you may be entitled to benefits with the help of a Chevy lemon law attorney. In this post, we discuss a few of the common Chevy Cruze car complaints, and what you can do about them.

Let’s get moving.

## Major Chevy Cruze Recalls

- Power Train

This [recall affects 2019 Chevy Cruze](https://www.cars.com/research/chevrolet-cruze-2019/recalls/) models. The complaint stems from missing bolts on the start-stop accumulator end cap. This can potentially result in an oil leak from the transmission, which can lead to a loss of propulsion, and subsequently increase the risk of a crash. Transmission oil leaks can also increase the risk of fires near the ignition source.

General Motors will notify owners or [lessees](/blog/leased-car-lemon-law) affected by these Chevrolet Cruze problems and will replace the missing bolts free of charge.

- Service Brakes

General Motors issued a recall of 2019 rear braking system problems with Chevy Cruze models. The caliper pistons may not have sufficient coating. This can lead to gas pockets forming - which may reduce the performance of the service brakes.

GM will notify owners and lessees of affected models to remedy the braking system, free of charge.

It has been reported that the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration has also issued safety recalls for the following Chevy Cruze problems:

- Issues with visibility

- Fuel system failures

- Faulty exterior lighting

- Back seat reclining problems

## Most Common Chevy Cruze Car Complaints Which We Have Seen

- Overheating

Of all the Chevy Cruze common problems, overheating can be the most stressful. Most cases of overheating can be traced back to the radiator fan, the thermostat, or a leak. The underlying cause of engine overheating can be a difficult problem to diagnose. If you notice this in your Chevy Cruze, get in touch with the dealership to get the problem diagnosed - and hopefully resolved.

- Shakes

Slight vibrations are normal in most vehicles. But excessive shaking can indicate serious Chevy Cruze problems. Some vehicles may be affected at lower speeds and feel it in the steering wheel. However, some reports have indicated shaking coming from under the vehicle - which can lead to a host of problems.

If you notice extreme shaking in your Chevy Cruze, get off the road as soon as you can and take it to the dealership for examination.

- Engine Problems

One of the most common Chevy Cruze car complaints which we have seen through the years relates to the check engine light. The check engine light can come on for many different reasons. However, if you notice your check engine light is flashing, it’s a sign that the engine is misfiring. This can be a big safety concern and must be sorted out immediately with the dealership.

- Air Bags

Chevy Cruze issues with the airbags aren’t always apparent until it’s too late. Unfortunately, the [Chevy Cruze model](https://chevyguide.com/chevy-cruze-years-to-avoid/) has not been immune from problems with the airbag. If the airbag light comes on in your Cruze, do not waste any time in getting it inspected. Of all the car problems you can have, this is one you never want to deal with in hindsight.

## What to Do if Your Chevy Cruze is Defective

If you notice any Chevy Cruze issues while your vehicle is under warranty, get in touch with the dealership immediately. A representative will instruct you on the next steps. This will likely involve taking your Cruze to a manufacturer-certified facility to begin the warranty repairs. In the scenario they aren’t able to fix the defect, you may be [entitled to benefits](/blog/defective-vehicle-purchase-rights) under the lemon law in California.

Your vehicle must meet the following qualifications to be eligible for benefits:

- The Cruze is covered under the original or dealer warranty; and

- The Cruze’s defect impairs the vehicle’s use, value, or safety; and

- The Cruze’s defect was not caused by neglect or abuse by the driver; and

- Chevrolet’s certified repair facility has been given a reasonable number of attempts to repair the defect (usually two or more); or

- The Cruze has been out of service for repairs for 30 or more total days.

Assuming your Chevrolet Cruze problems meet these guidelines, you may be entitled to a full buyback (refund) or replacement of the vehicle.

